 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Julie Kegels is feeling the weight of expectation this season. She just won the 2026 LVMH Prize, and throughout the six months of the competition, her professional life became a lot fuller. “There are more conversations, more opportunities, and more responsibilities,” she said before today’s show. But rather than lament the pressures of her newfound recognition, she decided to reframe the heaviness. “If something has weight, it also has value,” she said. And so, Kegels became fascinated with gravity this season, and explored how, with clever manipulation, you can mimic or amplify its effect on clothes.</p><p>As the first look appeared, a gray trouser with an extremely elongated crotch down to the knees, paired with a silk shirt and neon orange shoes, the idea became clear. “I wanted to explore falling in all its forms,” Kegels said. The runway included portions of stretch fabric, suspended just above the ground, to show the weight of each step. (It was inspired by a YouTube video of science educator Dan Burns, who used a similar setup to explain Einstein’s theory of general relativity.)</p><p>Dresses appeared in two halves, like the bulk had been ripped off and dragged down by a strong gravitational pull, leaving just the neckline intact (in-built nude or white bodysuits concealed the midriff). Denim jorts and navy suit trousers were undone and seemingly falling down, but secretly fixed to white or navy shirts by internal buttons, using an 18th-century fastening technique Kegels found when researching at her local library. Floral patterns sloped downwards, as if the fabric was being pulled while they were being embroidered. Organza prom dresses and skirts, sitting below the bust or at the hip, were sewn to super fine organza bodysuits.</p><p>We might not all want to walk around with our trousers undone. But there was plenty to wear in this collection. Perhaps the most effective use of gravity was an elegant silver tablecloth weight, dangling at the hip of a midnight organza dress, creating an asymmetrical pull and draping effect. The sequin bra tops were another highlight, inspired by a vintage bra Kegels found at a flea market, constructed without cups, leaving just the embellished straps and outline behind. They were layered on top of the nude bodysuits, with matching “falling” dresses or skirts attached inches below. These looks give the illusion of some pretty intricate styling in just one fell swoop.</p><p>Some of the simpler looks, like a pink cotton shirt and straight leg jeans, featured two or three lacquered tote and barrel bags in beige or brown, reminiscent of a busy woman on her daily commute, carrying heavy bags full of different looks for the office, a dinner and the gym. Kegels plans to invest in shoes and bags with her LVMH prize money, so we can expect more variation on this front in seasons to come.</p><p>The lab coats in the collection were a nod to Newton and his peers, sure. But also a nod to Kegels herself, who loves to experiment with how women wear clothes, from her quick change magic tricks (spring ’26) to last season’s shadowplay. For this spring, her party trick was sequins covered in fine pigment, trapped between layers of organza on skirts, that fell down through the fabric as the models walked, depositing blue or purple pigment on their shoes. “The models pulled on a transparent thread, which sent the pigment down, down, down,” Kegels said with a smile. “I wanted it to leave a trace.” In other looks, pink or purple glitter and sequins without pigment gathered in the bottom of fine organza balloon pants or sleeves, again creating shape with their weight.</p><p>Delphine Arnault said Kegels won the LVMH Prize partly because of how clearly she was able to tell her brand story. It will be interesting to see how the story develops, as her star continues to rise.</p></div>",
